          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have osteoarthritis in both knees.",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is a long-term condition where the cushioning in a joint wears down over time. \"Bilateral\" means it affects both knees. The reference material says it is manageable over long periods and does not inevitably lead to joint replacement. (Source: *Osteoarthritis*, general clinical reference)\n\n**Management:** The main treatment is structured exercise, and guidance places it ahead of medication. The program combines muscle strengthening around the knee, range-of-movement work, and aerobic activity. Guidance recommends starting with supervision, because the right movements and gradual progression matter. Weight management also helps where it is relevant. Mild discomfort during exercise within tolerable limits is not a sign of damage, and resting a painful knee is not recommended. (Sources: *Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management*; *Exercising with osteoarthritis*)\n\n**Classification:** In ICD-10-CM, the diagnosis code system used in the US, osteoarthritis sits in the M15 to M19 block. Codes in this system distinguish right, left, bilateral and unspecified sides. My reference material does not list the exact code for bilateral knee osteoarthritis, so please ask a clinician or medical coder to confirm it. (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 13*)\n\nMy lifestyle lookup did not complete, so I cannot give you a full list of diet, sleep, or activity recommendations beyond the exercise and weight points above.\n\nIf you have specific symptoms, such as pain patterns, swelling, stiffness, trouble walking, or questions about exercise, please share them. I can explain what the guidance says about them, and a clinician can advise on your individual situation. If you develop sudden severe swelling, redness, warmth, or a knee that gives way, seek medical care promptly.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
